  "headline": "Oil shock meets 5% yields: a stagflationary tilt that risk assets refuse to price",
  "body": [
    "The configuration is an energy-led inflation scare sitting on top of an economy that is still growing. Brent is up 3.9% on the day and 80% year-to-date, while the 10-year Treasury yield sits near 5.1%, almost a full point above where it started the year. Yet gold is pinned near its 52-week low and bitcoin is outrunning it, so markets are pricing a supply shock as an inflation and real-rate problem, not as a reason to hide.",
    "The interplay makes the case. Copper is up 16.5% year-to-date and the copper/gold ratio has risen 5% over the month, so the growth metal is beating the fear metal. Bitcoin's 28% three-month gain, with the ounces of gold one bitcoin buys up 10% over the month, says liquidity and risk appetite are intact. Gold's 3% monthly fall against a 5% nominal yield is the real-rate channel at work: with the dollar index near the top of its yearly range, bullion has no bid even with missiles in the air.",
    "This week cuts the other way and deserves naming. Treasury yields fell about 15 basis points while Brent rose 5%, and copper slipped 2%: the bond market is starting to treat crude as a tax on growth rather than a pure inflation impulse. The three-month trend, with yields up over 50 basis points alongside oil, still carries more weight, but a flat 30-year minus 10-year spread of 34 basis points shows no premium for reacceleration. The read is a squeeze, not a boom.",
    "The news bites through the oil channel. MintPress News reports Washington and Israel drafting a fresh strike on Iran with Saudi airspace closing under missile threats, and separately claims Yemen's Ansarallah now controls the Bab al-Mandab strait; Drop Site News reports Ukraine resuming strikes on Russian refineries and an EU-Gulf summit postponed amid attacks on Saudi airports. Taken as the outlets present them, every item points at the same choke points. That is bullish for Brent, with today's 4% jump suggesting the market is pricing escalation rather than resolution, and a headwind for long-dated Treasuries as energy pass-through keeps inflation sticky.",
    "The currency board confirms the read and flags one outlier. USD/CAD fell 0.5% today as the loonie rode the crude bid, and the dollar index slipped 0.3% despite the geopolitics, so the dollar is not drawing haven flows; a sustained oil bid stays supportive for CAD. The rupee, an oil importer's currency, is up almost 2% on the week, the one signal that does not fit; Brent near 110 is a headwind for INR and that strength is swimming against the energy tide."
  ],
